# Cursor Rules — Small Business Accounting & Inventory App

This project has a fully approved architecture (Modules 1–7, see /docs). These
rules exist so AI-assisted code in this repo never silently drifts from it.
When in doubt, check /docs before generating code — don't guess.

## Non-negotiable structural rules

1. **Stock is never a stored quantity.** Current stock for an item is always
   `SUM(quantity_remaining)` across its `stock_batches` rows. Never add a
   `quantity` column to the items table or any equivalent shortcut.

2. **FIFO costing only.** When a sale consumes stock, always deplete the
   oldest unconsumed batch first. COGS for a sale line comes from the actual
   batch(es) consumed (`sale_item_batch_consumption`), never from an
   averaged or estimated cost.

3. **Price override is one-way.** `sale_items.unit_price` may differ from
   `items.master_selling_price` (a per-transaction override), but nothing
   ever writes an override back into the item's master price.

4. **Staff must never see cost/COGS data.** No query, view, or UI surface
   reachable by a `staff`-role user may expose `purchase_cost_per_unit`,
   `unit_cogs`, `total_cogs`, or anything profit/margin-derived. Use the
   `*_staff_view` views (Module 7 §11) for staff-facing reads — never the
   base tables.

5. **Tenant isolation via `business_id`, enforced by RLS — not client code.**
   Every operational table is scoped by `business_id`. Never rely on the
   Flutter app to filter tenant data "for safety" — assume a hostile client
   and require the database to enforce it. When adding a new table, always
   add `business_id` and a matching RLS policy in the same change.

6. **Subscription status is never client-writable.** Only the Chapa webhook
   Edge Function (using the service_role key) may change
   `subscriptions.status` or insert `subscription_payments`. Never add a
   client-side path that marks a tenant as paid/active.

7. **Offline-first, always.** Every write in `features/*/data/*_repository.dart`
   must write to the local Drift database first and queue a sync operation —
   never make a write depend on a live network call succeeding.

8. **Platform-conditional storage — don't merge the two backends.**
   `core/database/connection/connection_native.dart` (mobile, SQLCipher
   encrypted) and `connection_web.dart` (web, WASM/OPFS, unencrypted — an
   accepted documented limitation) are selected via conditional import in
   `app_database.dart`. Don't add platform-specific logic anywhere else.

9. **Domain logic lives in `domain/`, not in widgets or Supabase.** FIFO
   calculation, price-override validation, and tier-limit checks are Dart
   functions in each feature's `domain/` folder — reusable, unit-testable,
   and identical whether online or offline.

## Project structure

- Feature-first: `lib/features/<feature>/{data,domain,presentation}/`
- Shared infrastructure only in `lib/core/` — don't put feature-specific
  logic there "because it felt important."
- Riverpod providers live next to what they expose (e.g. `itemRepositoryProvider`
  inside `item_repository.dart`), not in a separate global providers folder.

## Localization

- Every user-facing string goes in `lib/l10n/app_en.arb` first, then must
  also be added to `app_am.arb` (Amharic) and `app_om.arb` (Afan Oromo) in
  the same change — never ship an English-only string and "translate later."
- Flag any new Amharic/Afan Oromo translation as best-effort in a PR
  description if you're not a fluent speaker — don't present it as verified.

## Multi-tenant SaaS specifics

- Tiers: Basic (1 staff, standard reports) vs Pro (unlimited staff, custom
  date ranges, trend charts, PDF/Excel export). Check `subscription_tiers`
  feature flags — don't hardcode tier logic in the UI layer.
- Super Admin access to tenant data is consent-based and time-limited
  (`support_access_sessions`) — never build a standing/unrestricted
  Super Admin data-access path, even for convenience during debugging.

## Before implementing a new feature

1. Check `/docs/Module3_UserRoles_UseCases.md` for the relevant use case
   and permission matrix row.
2. Check `/docs/Module4_Database_Design.md` for the tables involved.
3. Check `/docs/Implementation_Roadmap.md` for which phase this belongs to
   — don't build ahead of dependencies (e.g., sales before stock batches).